text: <|channel|>analysis<|message|>Explain worst-case O(n) due to collisions, poor hash function, high load factor, rehashing, etc. Also mention that in practice, with good hash function and load factor, lookups remain O(1). Provide details.<|end|><|start|>assistant<|channel|>final<|message|>### When a hash table can become slow

Even though a hash table is designed for *constant‑time* lookups, there are several situations in which the time can grow and, in the worst case, become linear in the number of stored items.

| Situation | Why it slows down | Typical impact |
|-----------|-------------------|----------------|
| **Too many collisions** | All keys hash to the same bucket (or to a very small set of buckets). | The bucket becomes a long list (or a large cluster in open addressing). Lookup must scan that list → **O(n)**. |
| **Poor hash function** | The hash function distributes keys unevenly (e.g., always returns the
